---
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
import { displayDate } from '../../lib/format';
import { P, type SourceKey } from '../../lib/engine/params';
import { helpers } from '../../lib/helpers';
const lang = 'en';
const h = helpers(true);
const { N, m, num, pc } = h;
const L = (k: SourceKey, t?: string) => `<a href="${P.sources[k].url}" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">${t ?? P.sources[k].label.en}</a>`;
---
<PageLayout title="Degree by Credit Method: IPEDS and Scorecard Files, Limits" description="How Degree by Credit turns the federal IPEDS and College Scorecard files into prices per credit, degree totals and award rates, and what the files leave out." lang={lang} jsonLd={[articleSchema({ title: 'How the figures are built', description: 'Method of Degree by Credit', path: route('method', lang), lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path: route('method', lang) }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <header class="mb-6"><p class="kicker">Method</p><h1 class="mt-1 text-3xl font-bold text-navy-900 md:text-4xl">Degree by Credit method: the files, the rules and the limits</h1></header>
  <div class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <p>Every table and calculator on Degree by Credit is built from public federal files, downloaded on {displayDate(P.retrieved_at, 'en-US')} without an account or a key, and processed by one script, <code>scripts/build-data.py</code>, which can be run again from scratch. The calculators call one engine of pure functions, tested on every build. No figure is typed into a page. The files are rebuilt by hand when the Department of Education publishes new ones; nothing is fetched while you read, and no update schedule is promised.</p>

    <h2>The files</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>IPEDS directory, {P.ipeds.headerFile}</strong> (<Fragment set:html={L('ipeds_hd')} />): name, state, sector, level, category and Title IV status of each college.</li>
      <li><strong>IPEDS charges, {P.ipeds.chargesFile}</strong> (<Fragment set:html={L('ipeds_ic_ay')} />): per-credit charges for part-time undergraduates and graduates (district, state and out-of-state), full-time tuition and required fees, academic year {P.ipeds.chargesYear}. Variable definitions in <Fragment set:html={L('ipeds_ic_ay_dict')} />.</li>
      <li><strong>IPEDS distance education, {P.ipeds.distanceFile}</strong> (<Fragment set:html={L('ipeds_ef_dist')} />): students enrolled exclusively, partly or not at all in distance education, {P.ipeds.enrollmentTerm}.</li>
      <li><strong>College Scorecard, most recent cohorts institution file</strong>, released {displayDate(P.scorecard.release, 'en-US')} (<Fragment set:html={L('scorecard_data')} />): accreditor (as of {displayDate(P.scorecard.accreditorAsOf, 'en-US')}), eight-year award rate of students who entered in {P.scorecard.awardCohort}, Pell share and average net price ({P.scorecard.pellYear}), median debt ({P.scorecard.debtYears}) and median earnings ({P.scorecard.earningsNote}). Cohort years from <Fragment set:html={L('scorecard_dict')} />.</li>
      <li><strong>IPEDS programs offered via distance education, {P.programs.file}</strong> (<Fragment set:html={L('ipeds_c_dep')} />, {P.programs.release}, read on {displayDate(P.programs.read, 'en-US')}): for each college and field code of the <Fragment set:html={L('nces_cip')} />, the programs offered at each award level and those that can be completed entirely via distance education, completions year {P.programs.completionsYear}. Variable definitions in <Fragment set:html={L('ipeds_c_dep_dict')} />. Built by <code>scripts/build-programs.py</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>{P.ipeds.nextFilesStatus}.</p>
    <p>Field pages (nursing, MBA, accounting, law and others): {P.programs.rule} No program is ranked: lists are sorted by price per credit or by the number of programs completable online, and say so.</p>

    <h2>Which colleges are counted</h2>
    <p>A college is counted when the directory lists it in the 50 states or the District of Columbia, as degree-granting, active and taking part in the federal student aid programs (Title IV). That gives the whole federal list, online or not. This site keeps the {num(N.nOnline)} with at least 50 students enrolled exclusively in distance education, its “online colleges”: every median and every count on the site is read on them unless a sentence names another group; the cheapest lists ask for 500. “Fully online students” are those the college reports as enrolled exclusively in distance education courses.</p>

    <h2>Prices</h2>
    <p>The price per credit is the in-state charge for part-time undergraduates (HRCHG2) unless a page says otherwise; out-of-state is HRCHG3, district HRCHG1, graduate HRCHG6 and HRCHG7. A charge of zero or less is treated as not reported. Required fees are reported per year for a full-time student; to compare colleges on one figure, the “price per credit with fees” adds a thirtieth of the yearly fees to the per-credit charge, 30 credits being a full-time year. Colleges that charge a flat price per term report no per-credit charge: they are left out of per-credit rankings, and where they appear (Western Governors University, for example) their flat yearly tuition is used at 30 credits a year.</p>
    <p>Medians are taken over the online colleges of each group that report the field: {num(N.nPub4)} public four-year colleges, {num(N.nPub2)} public two-year colleges, {num(N.nNpf4)} private nonprofit and {num(N.nFp4)} for-profit four-year colleges; with {num(N.nPriv2)} private two-year colleges they make the online colleges that report an in-state charge, the list the percentiles and shares are read on. Only one count of colleges is printed on the site, the {num(N.nOnline)} online colleges; the shares are read on those of them that publish a price per credit. State medians count every public college of the state, community colleges included.</p>

    <h2>Calculator rules</h2>
    <p>Credits left = credits of the program − accepted transfer credits. Tuition = credits left × price per credit. Years = credits left ÷ (credits per term × terms per year), and fees are counted for each year started, rounded up to the half year. Grants are subtracted per year, never below zero. The pace every calculator starts from is {P.defaults.creditsPerTerm} credits a term, {P.defaults.termsPerYear} terms a year: a bachelor’s in four years (master’s and program pages: 18 credits a year, said in each result). Program lengths ({P.credits.associate} credits for an associate degree, {P.credits.bachelor} for a bachelor’s, {P.credits.master} for a master’s) are starting values the reader changes, not data.</p>

    <h2>Completion</h2>
    <p>The award rate is the College Scorecard measure OMAWDP8_ALL: the share of all students who entered in {P.scorecard.awardCohort}, first-time or transfer, full-time or part-time, who received an award from the college within eight years. It is wider than the older graduation rate, which follows first-time full-time students only. Medians: {pc(N.medOm8Online, 0)} at colleges where at least half the students are fully online ({num(N.nOm8Online)} colleges), {pc(N.medOm8Campus, 0)} where fewer than a quarter are ({num(N.nOm8Campus)}).</p>

    <h2>Tests</h2>
    <p>Run on every build: degree totals recomputed by hand for a bachelor’s with and without transfer credits; fees counted by the half year; grants capped at the gross cost; the out-of-state gap; the budget-to-price formula; the percentile reading at both ends; the associate-then-bachelor route; flat-rate tuition. A failing test stops the build.</p>

    <h2>What Degree by Credit leaves out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>a separate online rate, when a college bills online programs differently from what it reports to IPEDS;</li>
      <li>program fees (nursing, engineering, education), books, software, proctored exams and graduation fees;</li>
      <li>tuition changes after {P.ipeds.chargesYear};</li>
      <li>transfer policies, which each college sets;</li>
      <li>the quality of teaching or of a program, which no federal file measures.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Licences: IPEDS data are published by NCES in the public domain (<Fragment set:html={L('nces_policy')} />); the citation asked for is “{P.nces.citation}”. College Scorecard data are used under CC BY 4.0 with attribution in the footer of every page. Median price at public universities for state residents, for reference: {m(N.medPub4)} a credit.</p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
